{:edb/db-before-t 6135 :edb/db-after-t 6136 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539448 50 #inst "2026-10-24T01:55:53.869Z" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670013 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign $(88)$ as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}=\\frac{88÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{22}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $88%=\\frac{22}{25}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670013 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign $(88)$ as a fraction with denominator $100:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{88}{100}=\\frac{88÷4}{100÷4}=\\frac{22}{25}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $88\\%=\\frac{22}{25}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670024 1071 "Express $5%$ as a fraction in its simplest form.\n\n$5%=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670024 1071 "Express $5\\%$ as a fraction in its simplest form.\n\n$5\\%=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670024 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($5$) as a fraction with denominator $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}=\\frac{5÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5%=\\frac{1}{20}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670024 1074 "First, we write the number in front of the percent sign ($5$) as a fraction with denominator $100$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}\n$$\n\nWe now simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $5$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{100}=\\frac{5÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{1}{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\%=\\frac{1}{20}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670029 1074 "First, we write $\\frac{7}{25}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{25}=\\frac{7\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{28}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{100}=28%\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670029 1074 "First, we write $\\frac{7}{25}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{25}=\\frac{7\\times 4}{25\\times 4}=\\frac{28}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{28}{100}=28\\%\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670038 1071 "A sum of $\\$2,000$ is deposited in a bank. It earns simple interest of $5%$ per year. After several years, the final balance is $\\$2,400.$ How long was the deposit held in the bank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670038 1071 "A sum of $\\$2,000$ is deposited in a bank. It earns simple interest of $5\\%$ per year. After several years, the final balance is $\\$2,400.$ How long was the deposit held in the bank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670038 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the interest rate is $5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n5%=5÷100=0.05\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,400-2,000=400\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$400.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 400 & =2,000\\times 0.05\\times n \\\\ 400 & =100n \\\\ n & =\\frac{400}{100} \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $4$ years." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670038 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the interest rate is $5\\%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n5\\%=5÷100=0.05\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n2,400-2,000=400\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$400.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 400 & =2,000\\times 0.05\\times n \\\\ 400 & =100n \\\\ n & =\\frac{400}{100} \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $4$ years." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670041 1074 "First, we write $\\frac{23}{50}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{23}{50}=\\frac{23\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{46}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{46}{100}=46%\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670041 1074 "First, we write $\\frac{23}{50}$ as an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{23}{50}=\\frac{23\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{46}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{46}{100}=46\\%\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670043 1071 "Jamie deposited a principal of $\\$4,000$ in his bank account where it will earn $8.5%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $8$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670043 1071 "Jamie deposited a principal of $\\$4,000$ in his bank account where it will earn $8.5\\%$ simple interest per year. What will be the final balance after $8$ years?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670043 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$4,000,$ the rate is $8.5%$ per year, and the time is $8$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n8.5%=8.5÷100=0.085\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =4,000\\times 0.085\\times 8 \\\\  & =2,720.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,720.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n4,000+2,720=6,720\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$6,720.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670043 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$4,000,$ the rate is $8.5\\%$ per year, and the time is $8$ years. \n\nConverting the rate to a decimal, we have:\n\n$$\n8.5\\%=8.5÷100=0.085\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for simple interest, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\  & =4,000\\times 0.085\\times 8 \\\\  & =2,720.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,720.$ To find the final balance, we add the interest amount to the principal:\n\n$$\n4,000+2,720=6,720\n$$\n\nTherefore, the final balance will be $\\$6,720.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670069 1074 "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $8$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n3,280-2,000=1,280\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,280.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,280 & =2,000\\times r\\times 8 \\\\ 1,280 & =16,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,280}{16,000} \\\\ r & =0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.08$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.08=0.08\\times 100%=8%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $8%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670069 1074 "The principal is $\\$2,000,$ and the time is $8$ years. To find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n3,280-2,000=1,280\n$$\n\nSo the interest amount is $\\$1,280.$ \n\nLet $r$ be the interest rate. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 1,280 & =2,000\\times r\\times 8 \\\\ 1,280 & =16,000r \\\\ r & =\\frac{1,280}{16,000} \\\\ r & =0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, converting the rate $r=0.08$ to a percent, we get\n\n$$\n0.08=0.08\\times 100\\%=8\\%.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interest rate is $8\\%.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670084 1071 "Mitch deposited $\\$6,000$ in his bank account. It earns simple interest of $8.5%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$8,550.$ How long did Mitch hold the deposit in the bank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670084 1071 "Mitch deposited $\\$6,000$ in his bank account. It earns simple interest of $8.5\\%$ per year. After some years, the final balance was $\\$8,550.$ How long did Mitch hold the deposit in the bank?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670084 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$6,000,$ and the interest rate is $8.5%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n8.5%=8.5÷100=0.085\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n8,550-6,000=2,550\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,550.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 2,550 & =6,000\\times 0.085\\times n \\\\ 2,550 & =510n \\\\ n & =\\frac{2,550}{510} \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $5$ years." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670084 1074 "Here, the principal is $\\$6,000,$ and the interest rate is $8.5\\%$ per year. Converting the interest rate to a decimal, we get\n\n$$\n8.5\\%=8.5÷100=0.085\\,.\n$$\n\nTo find the interest amount, we subtract the principal from the final balance:\n\n$$\n8,550-6,000=2,550\n$$\n\nSo the amount of interest earned is $\\$2,550.$ \n\nLet $n$ be the number of years for which the deposit was held in the bank. Using the formula, then, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Simple Interest} & =\\text{Principal}\\times \\text{Rate}\\times \\text{Time} \\\\ 2,550 & =6,000\\times 0.085\\times n \\\\ 2,550 & =510n \\\\ n & =\\frac{2,550}{510} \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the deposit was held in the bank for $5$ years." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670321 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a Bernoulli distribution?\n\nI. Recording whether a soccer player scores a penalty\n\nII. The number of victories a chess player obtains in $4$ matches given that the probability of the player winning any individual game is $60%$\n\nIII. Recording whether a randomly chosen person in a population has blood type A$-$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670321 1071 "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a Bernoulli distribution?\n\nI. Recording whether a soccer player scores a penalty\n\nII. The number of victories a chess player obtains in $4$ matches given that the probability of the player winning any individual game is $60\\%$\n\nIII. Recording whether a randomly chosen person in a population has blood type A$-$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670323 1071 "It is known that $7%$ of a population has blood type O$-.$ What is the probability that the next donor in a blood center has blood type O$-?$ Assume donors are chosen at random from the population, and the situation can be modeled as a Bernoulli trial." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670323 1071 "It is known that $7\\%$ of a population has blood type O$-.$ What is the probability that the next donor in a blood center has blood type O$-?$ Assume donors are chosen at random from the population, and the situation can be modeled as a Bernoulli trial." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670323 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ represent the donor's blood type: $X=1$ if the donor has blood type O$-$ and $X=0$ if the donor hasn't. We wish to compute $P(X=1).$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that the donor has blood type O$-$ with probability $p=7%=0.07.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07).$\n\nIf $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.07, & x=1 \\\\ 0.93, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next donor has blood type O$-$ is\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.07.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670323 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ represent the donor's blood type: $X=1$ if the donor has blood type O$-$ and $X=0$ if the donor hasn't. We wish to compute $P(X=1).$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that the donor has blood type O$-$ with probability $p=7\\%=0.07.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07).$\n\nIf $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}p, & x=1 \\\\ 1-p, & x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.07),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.07, & x=1 \\\\ 0.93, & x=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next donor has blood type O$-$ is\n\n$$\nP(X=1)=f(1)=0.07.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670325 1071 "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $55%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the expected value of $X.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670325 1071 "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $55\\%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the expected value of $X.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670325 1074 "From the problem statement, we know that the probability that the player will score is $p=55%=0.55.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.55).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=p.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.55.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670325 1074 "From the problem statement, we know that the probability that the player will score is $p=55\\%=0.55.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.55).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=p.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=0.55.\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670328 1071 "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $42%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the standard deviation of $X.$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670328 1071 "The final of a soccer tournament goes to \"sudden death\" penalties. The first player to score a penalty wins. A player that has scored $42\\%$ of his previous penalties steps up to take a penalty. Let $X$ represent the outcome of the penalty: $X=1$ if the player scores and $X=0$ if he does not. By modeling the situation as a Bernoulli trial, determine the standard deviation of $X.$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186670328 1074 "From the problem statement, we know that the player scores the penalty with probability $p=42%=0.42.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.42).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{p(1-p)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{(0.42)(0.58)}≈0.494\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186670328 1074 "From the problem statement, we know that the player scores the penalty with probability $p=42\\%=0.42.$ So, we have $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(0.42).$ \n\nWe wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$ In general, if $X∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p),$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{p(1-p)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{(0.42)(0.58)}≈0.494\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
